    <DataNode elementId="AT5G39050-MONOMER" textLabel="Phenolic glucoside malonyltransferase 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q940Z5" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">PMaT1 is a phenolic glucoside malonyltransferase based on the analysis of the metabolic phenotype of a &lt;i&gt;pmat1&lt;/i&gt; mutant as well as &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays |CITS: [20626660]|. In these assays, PMaT1 showed high activity with |FRAME: CPD-13683 2-naphthol glucoside| (100%) and slightly higher activity with |FRAME: CPD-8007 kaempferol 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside| (108%) (with a K&lt;sub&gt;m&lt;/sub&gt; of 31 &amp;micro;M and a k&lt;sub&gt;cat&lt;/sub&gt; of 33 sec&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;). It showed less activity with |FRAME: CPD-13686 1-naphthol glucoside| (78%), |FRAME: CPD1F-453 kaempferol-3-glucoside| (68%), and |FRAME: CPD-3424 daidzin| (42%). Very low activity was observed using |FRAME: CPD-11641 4-methylumbelliferyl glucoside| (13%) or |FRAME: SCOPOLIN scopolin| (8%) as the substrate. And, no activity could be detected against |FRAME: CPD-8034 kaempferol 3,7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-diglucoside|, |FRAME: CPD-12628 salicylate 2-O-&amp;beta;-D-glucoside|, or |FRAME: CPD-12447 phlorizin| |CITS: [20626660]|. Additional kinetic details are provided with individual reactions below. The activty profile and substrate specificity of PMaT1 differed from that observed with the related PMaT2 enzyme |CITS: [20626660]|. In Arabidopsis, phenolic xenobiotics such as naphthols are regularly glucosylated in order to help detoxify them. They may then be exported from the cell as glucosides. However, malonyltranferases, such as PMaT1 can further modify these compounds. If this directs them for storage in the vacuole, it would be expected to serve as a protective modification, however, it appears that germinating seeds over-expressing PMAT1 are more sentive to |FRAME: CPD-8131 2-naphthol|, perhaps due to a build-up of |FRAME: CPD-13685 2-naphthol 6&apos;-O-malonylglucoside| in the cytosol. Therefore the role of PMaT1-mediated xenobiotic glucoside malonylation in plant growth requires further investigation. It is also possible that PMaT1 could act on endogenous phenolic glucosides found in Arabidopsis. |CITS: [20626660]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G39050-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G39050.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q940Z5" />
